WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.230 --> 00:00:04.330
How many friends do you have?

00:00:04.330 --> 00:00:07.080
How many friends would you like to have?

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:12.370
A better question would be how many friends
can you have?

00:00:12.370 --> 00:00:18.120
In the 90s British Anthropologist, Robin Dunbar
looked at the correlation between the brain

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:22.480
size of different primates and the size of
their social groups.

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:28.480
By extrapolating these numbers to the human
brain size Dunbar postulated that the maximum

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:34.750
social relationships any human can maintain
is around 150.

00:00:34.750 --> 00:00:40.090
Specifically, Dunbar found that the larger
a species’ neocortex, the top part of the

00:00:40.090 --> 00:00:45.140
brain, the larger social groups that species
can maintain.

00:00:45.140 --> 00:00:51.260
This is because each human friendship requires
the dedication of a portion of one’s resources.

00:00:51.260 --> 00:00:56.750
The resource of time required to meet with
them occasionally so they don’t forget you

00:00:56.750 --> 00:00:59.190
exist or vice versa.

00:00:59.190 --> 00:01:04.810
The resource of memory, you have to remember
a huge amount of details about each one of

00:01:04.810 --> 00:01:10.010
your friends, foods they do and don’t eat,
what type of films and music they like, what

00:01:10.010 --> 00:01:15.950
angers them, what makes them laugh and cry,
what they do for a living, who their partner

00:01:15.950 --> 00:01:19.070
or spouse is, who their friends are.

00:01:19.070 --> 00:01:24.170
And then there’s the resource of emotional
support, at some point, all friends will call

00:01:24.170 --> 00:01:30.080
upon you for your emotional support in times
of need, we all have a limited amount of support

00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:36.060
and friendly advice to go around, expend too
much of it and you will start to feel like

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:37.100
a shrink.

00:01:37.100 --> 00:01:43.720
Put all these factors together and you start
to see how maintaining more than 150 relationships

00:01:43.720 --> 00:01:47.170
isn’t possible for any human.

00:01:47.170 --> 00:01:53.030
Some people can have a few more, or somewhat
fewer, but 150 is the general limit imposed

00:01:53.030 --> 00:01:56.180
by our brains, our biology.

00:01:56.180 --> 00:02:01.250
Look at the world around you and you will
start to notice Dunbar’s number everywhere.

00:02:01.250 --> 00:02:07.280
Anthropologists believe the largest tribal
groups in our hunter-gatherer days were 150

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:13.879
and it held true thousands of years later,
The Doomsday Book showed that the average

00:02:13.879 --> 00:02:17.379
village size in England was 150.

00:02:17.379 --> 00:02:21.049
The average number of wedding guests is 141.

00:02:21.049 --> 00:02:27.969
150 may be how many relationships one can
have but in reality, there is a much, much

00:02:27.969 --> 00:02:34.659
smaller limit on how many close friends you
can have - which may be cause for relief if

00:02:34.659 --> 00:02:41.450
you’re sat there thinking “Wait, I’m
supposed to have 150 friends, I have 2”

00:02:41.450 --> 00:02:48.140
well calm down two is absolutely plenty because
in truth the limit is just five.

00:02:48.140 --> 00:02:53.379
In 2007 Robin Dunbar was back at it again,
proving we’re all terrible at knowing lots

00:02:53.379 --> 00:02:54.379
of people.

00:02:54.379 --> 00:02:59.760
By examining the calls logs from the phones
of 27,000 Europeans he deducted that people

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:06.159
are realistically capable of having five close
friendships, including intimate relations.

00:03:06.159 --> 00:03:13.709
In fact, for the average Joe 80% of all their
calls are made to the same four people.

00:03:13.709 --> 00:03:21.840
But Thoughty2 I have 325 friends on Facebook
and my mate Dave has over 700, but I secretly

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:23.200
think he’s a prick.

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:28.480
Well, you’ve just nicely illustrated my
next point - no you don’t, because they’re

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:30.310
not your friends.

00:03:30.310 --> 00:03:34.709
Facebook friends are no more than numbers
on a server, go through your friend list and

00:03:34.709 --> 00:03:41.469
you’ll notice most of them are people you’ve
met once and will probably never see again,

00:03:41.469 --> 00:03:47.620
simply friends of someone you once worked
with or most likely people who are supposed

00:03:47.620 --> 00:03:51.239
to be your friend, but you secretly hate their
guts.

00:03:51.239 --> 00:03:56.019
None of these peoples counts as friends - I
wish I was sorry to disappoint you, but I’m

00:03:56.019 --> 00:04:00.249
not - because if you genuinely think they
are all close friends and not just casual

00:04:00.249 --> 00:04:06.079
acquaintances then you have truly undervalued
the close bond of a genuine friendship.

00:04:06.079 --> 00:04:10.819
You cannot truly consider someone a friend
unless you converse with them face to face

00:04:10.819 --> 00:04:16.200
on a regular basis and you can rely on them
to help you out in times of need and vice

00:04:16.200 --> 00:04:17.200
versa.

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:21.920
Now, if anything I’ve said has made you
doubt your relationships then you better strap

00:04:21.920 --> 00:04:26.370
yourself in Sonny because unfortunately, I’ve
got more bad news for you.

00:04:26.370 --> 00:04:33.150
You see, it’s statistically likely that
half your friends don’t even like you.

00:04:33.150 --> 00:04:41.220
A study at MIT analysed the relationships
between 84 so-called ‘friends’ aged 23

00:04:41.220 --> 00:04:42.510
to 38.

00:04:42.510 --> 00:04:47.890
Everyone was asked to rank everyone else in
the study between 0 and 5, 0 meaning ‘I

00:04:47.890 --> 00:04:52.100
don’t know this person’ and 5 meant ‘They’re
one of my best friends’.

00:04:52.100 --> 00:04:59.510
94 per cent of people expected their friendships
to be reciprocated, but astonishingly only

00:04:59.510 --> 00:05:03.150
53 per cent of them actually were.

00:05:03.150 --> 00:05:09.600
This means that half of the people who were
supposed to be a participant’s friend said

00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:14.210
they didn’t actually consider that participant
to be their friend.

00:05:14.210 --> 00:05:20.220
The sample size used in this study was very
small but other data sources containing 92,000

00:05:20.220 --> 00:05:28.350
individuals say the reciprocity rate of friendships
is between 34 to 53 per cent.

00:05:28.350 --> 00:05:30.760
This is rather sad.

00:05:30.760 --> 00:05:35.000
And gives one cause for concern, especially
when a study from Harvard University found

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:40.410
that having no friends can be just as damaging
to your health as smoking.

00:05:40.410 --> 00:05:43.940
But, there is a positive you can take away
here.

00:05:43.940 --> 00:05:47.830
You could cull half of your friends from your
life and probably be happier.

00:05:47.830 --> 00:05:54.070
This, of course, means you have to first know
who the false friends are, which is easier

00:05:54.070 --> 00:05:55.510
said than done.

00:05:55.510 --> 00:06:00.350
So before you rock up to work tomorrow and
tell everyone in HR to F’off - which you

00:06:00.350 --> 00:06:07.210
should totally do, by the way, it’s incredibly
cathartic - you should probably think it over.

00:06:07.210 --> 00:06:11.080
So how do you know who is only pretending
to be your friend?

00:06:11.080 --> 00:06:16.750
Well, there’s no secret technique here,
but one of the most obvious ways is to think

00:06:16.750 --> 00:06:20.900
whether that person has ever shared a secret
with you.

00:06:20.900 --> 00:06:26.740
Sharing a secret with another requires a huge
amount of trust which one would only do with

00:06:26.740 --> 00:06:30.970
someone they admire and respect as a friend.

00:06:30.970 --> 00:06:36.060
Also, when you are with with your group of
friends, are you always the one they ask to

00:06:36.060 --> 00:06:40.820
take the picture, if so then, sorry pal, it’s
probably you.

00:06:40.820 --> 00:06:46.740
However, it’s likely that it wasn’t always
this way and that reciprocity levels amongst

00:06:46.740 --> 00:06:51.870
friendship groups have dropped significantly
in recent decades.

00:06:51.870 --> 00:06:59.460
This is because the perceived value of a friendship
has dropped with the advent of social media.

00:06:59.460 --> 00:07:04.920
Today a new friendship can be created with
a tap or swipe and just as easily removed

00:07:04.920 --> 00:07:06.660
from our lives.

00:07:06.660 --> 00:07:12.910
In an online world driven by a vicious cycle
of instant gratification and game theory,

00:07:12.910 --> 00:07:18.420
friends are seen as little more than points
to collect in the game of life.

00:07:18.420 --> 00:07:22.610
When some people choose to accept a friend
request, the thought usually passes through

00:07:22.610 --> 00:07:27.310
their mind of “How will this make me look
better”.

00:07:27.310 --> 00:07:34.880
The problem is that this is anathema to why
homo sapiens evolved to form friendships.

00:07:34.880 --> 00:07:42.040
Why do you think our species out of all Hominids
came to dominate all others, why not chimpanzees

00:07:42.040 --> 00:07:45.290
or our once rivals neanderthal?

00:07:45.290 --> 00:07:48.370
After all they had larger craniums than sapiens.

00:07:48.370 --> 00:07:53.940
Well, it certainly wasn’t because of our
strength over other species, there were many

00:07:53.940 --> 00:07:59.760
species, bears, lions, chimpanzees and indeed
neanderthals that could tear our limbs off

00:07:59.760 --> 00:08:02.180
like pretty little chicken wings.

00:08:02.180 --> 00:08:07.610
And contrary to what you may think, it wasn’t
because of our large brains, if so elephants

00:08:07.610 --> 00:08:13.940
would be CEOs and politicians, no, even though
that is a rather nice image.

00:08:13.940 --> 00:08:20.330
Okay, it was because of our superior intelligence,
but it was something far more specific about

00:08:20.330 --> 00:08:27.220
our intelligence in particular - other species
were and still are extraordinarily intelligent.

00:08:27.220 --> 00:08:33.700
But humans dominated the playing field because
of the unique way homo sapiens’ brains evolved

00:08:33.700 --> 00:08:37.399
to be able to form complex social bonds.

00:08:37.399 --> 00:08:45.410
Other animals, bees, chimpanzees formed social
groups but none had the inherent ability to

00:08:45.410 --> 00:08:48.569
do so quite like homo sapiens.

00:08:48.569 --> 00:08:54.319
Homo sapiens can, and as far as we know this
is unique in the animal kingdom, gossip about

00:08:54.319 --> 00:09:01.230
others behind their back so tribesmen knew
who to avoid in their tribe and other tribes.

00:09:01.230 --> 00:09:09.149
And also, we can imagine things that we have
never seen before, like when a fellow tribesman

00:09:09.149 --> 00:09:13.730
described a beautiful waterfall near that
big pointy rock over there.

00:09:13.730 --> 00:09:19.040
Our ability to form these super complex social
groups and maintain knowledge on who doesn’t

00:09:19.040 --> 00:09:24.209
like whom, who is willing to protect us when
hunting and most importantly: who stole john’s

00:09:24.209 --> 00:09:25.209
rock?

00:09:25.209 --> 00:09:31.899
Are the reasons why homo sapiens were able
to work together to form groups that collectively

00:09:31.899 --> 00:09:37.920
were greater and many times more formidable
than their sum parts.

00:09:37.920 --> 00:09:43.730
Friendships allowed us to hunt in groups,
protect each other and look after our sick.

00:09:43.730 --> 00:09:50.420
So ask yourself, when you and your best friend
are together are you greater than your sum

00:09:50.420 --> 00:09:52.250
parts?

00:09:52.250 --> 00:09:58.040
Can you do amazing things together and forge
great memories?

00:09:58.040 --> 00:10:00.839
Could you rely on them in times of need?

00:10:00.839 --> 00:10:23.410
If not, it’s possible they could be on the
negative side of your 50%.

